Life predictions for components containing cracks or crack-like flaws require knowledge of the fatigue crack growth characteristics of the material. Ideally, the crack growth properties of the exact material undergoing the exact service load profile and environment would be readily available, but this is rarely the case. Therefore, it may be necessary to estimate crack growth rates based on material properties. Some methods have been developed to do exactly this, such as that proposed by Glinka 30  which formulates the constants in the Paris equation using through notch considerations and strain-controlled fatigue parameters. While Glinka’s method offers a mechanistically sound approximation method, it requires knowledge of the strain life and cyclic stress–strain curve of the material, fatigue crack growth threshold values, and is formulated in terms of the effective rather than applied stress intensity. While several models have been developed to account for deviations between the applied and effective stress intensity range due to crack closure, such as those proposed by Elber 31  and Schijve, 32  the additional constants are not generally available for cast steel literature data and their inclusion would convolute the present analysis. As such, the present analysis is restricted to the nominal or applied stress intensity range, and no attempt is made to quantify the degree of crack closure or discern the effective stress intensity range from the applied stress intensity range. Despite its simplicity and assumptions implicit in its formulation, the familiar original form of the Paris equation 33  is still widely used, given as da dN = C Δ K m \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
