Figure.  1  compares the fatigue crack growth data for ferrite-pearlite cast steels from the literature dataset against the Barsom ferrite-pearlite upper bound. While not encompassing all the data points, the Barsom ferrite-pearlite upper bound offers a reasonable approximation for the ferrite-pearlite cast steels, particularly within the range of stress intensities commonly encountered within engineering practice (region II crack growth). Some deviation occurs at the extremes of the stress intensity ranges, but the crack growth rate determined by the upper bound generally remains within 2x of the experimentally measured data points. Notably, the cast steel data in Figure  1  exhibit a steeper slope than that of the Barsom ferrite-pearlite upper bound, which utilizes a fatigue crack growth exponent (m-value) of 3. The steeper slope of the cast steel data is corroborated by the fatigue crack growth exponents tabulated in Table  1  and indicates a degree of increased crack growth rate sensitivity to the stress intensity range. Increased stress intensity range sensitivity was also noted explicitly by Caulfield for ASTM A27, 36 and while the trend also seems applicable for the broader range of ferrite-pearlite cast steels included here, the suitability of the Barsom ferrite-pearlite upper bound is still apparent. Figure 1 Fatigue crack growth data for various ferrite-pearlite cast steels compared to Barsom upper bound (solid green line). Note that the designations in the legend do not imply a ferrite-pearlite microstructure for a given grade/composition. Table 1 Tabulated Paris Constants for Cast Steels at Room Temperature in Air Alloy C m Source ASTM A27 65-35 2.97E-13 3.8 36 ASTM A216 WCC a 4.40E-12 a 3 39 SAE 0030 3.34E-14 4.33 27 1.53E-13 3.53 40 2.20E-13 3.82 41 7.61E-12 3.14 40 SAE 0050A 2.24E-13 3.88 27 1.99E-12 3.4 27 0.25C low carbon steel a 3.13E-12 a 3.3 42 b 2.03E-12 b 3.3 42 0.32C med. carbon/low-alloy steel a 3.13E-12 a 3.3 42 b 2.03E-12 b 3.3 42 ASTM A217