Knowledge of the fatigue crack growth threshold is required to determine whether an existing crack will propagate under cyclic loading. While fatigue crack growth thresholds can exhibit considerable variability and require significant experimental effort to obtain, their importance in the determination of non-propagating cracks and integration within current flaw assessment procedures warrants inclusion in the present study. Conservative threshold approximations have been developed for wrought steels and these guidelines form the basis of the following comparisons. Codified thresholds from the ASME BPVC Section VIII and XI included in the present study for cast steel comparisons were compiled from the extensive work of Hasegawa et al. 69 , 70 , and the reader is directed to these dedicated studies for an extensive review of threshold guidelines in a broad range of FFS codes. Guidance can also be found in fatigue and fracture literature, such as the fatigue crack growth threshold values proposed by Barsom for martensitic, bainitic, austenitic, and ferrite-pearlite pressure vessel steels operating in non-aggressive environments, Δ K th = 7 1 - 0.85 R M P a m R > 0.1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
