The use of the modified Neuber’s rule also requires the selection of an appropriate fatigue notch factor. It has been proposed that steel castings are less notch sensitive than comparable wrought steels 101 ,  102 – 103  but the authors are not aware of an equivalent Neuber or Peterson parameter that has been developed explicitly for steel castings that would allow a closed-form estimation of the notch sensitivity based on tensile strength. Alternatively, consideration of the specific notch sensitivity can be omitted, and the fatigue notch factor calculated directly through the use of the quantitative radiographic standard proposed by Blair et al. 104  and validated under monotonic loading by Hardin and Beckermann. 105  In the context of the present study, the maximum indication fraction is considered as the indication length perpendicular to the primary stress axis over the section thickness, i.e., 0.1 corresponds to radiographic indication spanning a maximum of 1/10 th  the section thickness and is referred to here as Level I severity. The standard proposed by Blair, while illustrated using radiography, could reasonably be applied to other NDE methods in which a quality detail size can be discerned such as phased array ultrasound (PAUT). Using the fatigue notch factors back-calculated by Blair et al. from the experimental data of Sigl et al., a closed-form relationship can be developed to estimate the fatigue notch factor for a given quality detail severity level as illustrated in Figure  10 . The intercept of the exponential function was forced to 1 such that Level 0 corresponds to a fatigue notch factor of 1, Figure 10 Fatigue notch factor vs. maximum radiographic indication fraction back-calculated from experimental fatigue data of 8630 cast steel 84  by Blair et al. 104  The exponential function fitted to the data to calculate a notch factor for increasing radiographic indication fractions is shown by the dotted black line. K f = exp ( 4.0341 ∗ F ) \documentcl